This matches the old PR's ctypes `argtypes`/`restype` exactly (`[c_vp, c_i, c_bool] -> c_vp`; `[c_vp, c_vp, c_d, c_vp, c_vp] -> None`; `[c_vp] -> None`) — **the old bindings are not stale**. From `proj_powsqrt_on_basis.c` (the implementation, read in full):
- `basis` is a **configuration-space** basis (`up->ndim = basis->ndim`, `up->num_basis = basis->num_basis`).
- **Single-field only**: `fIn`/`fOut` must have `ncomp == basis->num_basis` exactly (`fIn_d[k]`/`gkyl_array_fetch(fOut, linidx)` index `0..num_basis`, no field/component loop at all — unlike `gkyl_dg_mul_op` et al., there is no `c_oop`/field-index argument anywhere in this API). A multi-field caller must loop field-by-field in Python, the same convention `dg/modal.average` already uses for `gkyl_array_average` (§ dg/modal.py `average()`, lines 67–144, slicing `a.view().reshape(a.size, nfields, nb)`).
- **Negative-value clamp** (line 167 of `proj_powsqrt_on_basis.c`): `fOut_q[0] = (fIn_q < 0.) ? 1.e-40 : pow(sqrt(fIn_q), expIn);` — evaluated pointwise at each quadrature node, not checked against the DG coefficients; a field whose *coefficients* look positive can still dip negative between quadrature nodes and get silently clamped there. Worth guarding/documenting the same way `weak_inv` documents its Gkeyll-imposed limits.
- `num_quad` is quadrature nodes **per dimension** (`init_quad_values` builds an `ndim`-fold tensor-product Gauss-Legendre rule, `tot_quad = num_quad**ndim`), using precomputed tables when `num_quad <= gkyl_gauss_max`, else `gkyl_gauleg` on the fly — i.e. it is **not indexed into any fixed per-`(basis_type,ndim,poly_order)` kernel table** the way `gkyl_dg_mul_op`/`gkyl_dg_inv_op`/etc. are; there is no analogous `_WEAK_MAX_POLY_ORDER`-style guard needed in `kernels.py` beyond straightforward argument validation (`num_quad >= 1`, `ncomp == num_basis`), which is a meaningfully simpler guard story than every other kernel in that file.
- Confirmed by `/home/maxwell-rosen/postgkyl/gkeyll/core/unit/ctest_proj_powsqrt_on_basis.c`: call site convention is `gkyl_proj_powsqrt_on_basis_new(&basis, poly_order+1, use_gpu)` then `gkyl_proj_powsqrt_on_basis_advance(proj_up, &local, exponent, f, g)` — `range` is the *local* (non-ghost) range, matching `gpython_array_integrate`'s and `gpython_array_average`'s existing "lower=1, upper=cells, no ghost" range convention in `gpython.c`.

**Important cross-check**: `postgkyl.dg.rep.apply_pointwise` (`/home/maxwell-rosen/postgkyl/src/postgkyl/dg/rep.py:181-199`) already implements a *generically equivalent* operation — modal → Gauss-Legendre quadrature (via `basis.py`'s own `modal_to_quad_matrix`/`quad_to_modal_matrix`, which use the exact same tensor-product Gauss-Legendre rule) → arbitrary NumPy `fn` → project back — exposed as the public verb `operations.represent.apply(data, fn, num_quad=...)` / `GData.apply(fn, num_quad=...)`. A single-field `pow(sqrt(f), exponent)` with the same negative-clamp semantics is expressible **today, with zero new C code**, as:
